100
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:39,880
And how do we get patrols operating independent from the troop given the conditions that we're given right now.

101
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:43,420
So let's get started with that. shall we

102
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:47,800
Scoutmaster Ship in 7 Minutes or Less?

103
00:07:47,800 --> 00:08:04,640
Well, we've spent some time over the past several podcasts talking about applying the patrol system or method- kind of interchangeable terms- and strengthening your youth leadership.

104
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:11,380
Well, there are some important realities that we need to consider in doing these things,

105
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:21,060
And I'm especially concentrating here on the independent operation of patrols away from the organization of a troop.

106
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:27,200
One of the ways of understanding what I'm talking about here is to think a little bit about the history of scouting and the way scouting developed.

107
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:35,159
The principles of the patrol system when first introduced enabled scouts to operate in small groups and use their own initiative.

108
00:08:35,159 --> 00:08:42,740
I think what really appealed to boys and accounted for the phenomenal growth of scouting in its early years was the appeal of real adventure

109
00:08:43,459 --> 00:08:55,420
offered by a program, coupled with the challenge it offered to their patrol to plan its own activities and to be responsible for itself for discipline, without adult oversight and interference.

110
00:08:55,420 --> 00:09:09,120
The patrol system recognizes the natural desire in boys to form into gangs or groups or clubs- or whatever label you want to put on it- in their neighborhoods and schools.

111
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:16,360
And these kind of naturally forming gangs became the first patrols and started scouting.

112
00:09:17,459 --> 00:09:21,900
When the first scout patrols were formed there were no such thing as a scoutmaster.

113
00:09:22,959 --> 00:09:24,240
Think about that. now.

114
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:27,880
Boys got Baden-Powell's Scouting for Boys book.

115
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:29,140
They read about it,

116
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:30,900
They got excited about the concept.

117
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:32,280
They got their buddies together

118
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:33,560
And they started scouting.

119
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:41,880
They learned after a while that they would need some help and advice from someone older in order to carry out some of the more adventurous activities

120
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:47,880
And to be able to function, to do things. they were going to need a little bit of help

121
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:53,380
And this is the way that the role of scoutmaster came into existence:

122
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:58,080
Just to be an aide, an assistant, an advisor to a patrol of scouts.

123
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:03,920
In the first few decades of scouting, patrols were more or less arranged around neighborhoods.

124
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:09,420
Now, if you think back, you know in America we're looking at 1910.

125
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:11,240
Well, things have changed a little bit.

126
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:16,300
In that era a boy's friends and playmates were usually within walking distance of his home.

127
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:19,719
Now a boy's circle of friends is likely to be much farther away.

128
00:10:20,199 --> 00:10:21,939
Now it's really quite a bit different,

129
00:10:22,319 --> 00:10:28,620
Because things are different. this means that the members of a patrol can live a fair distance from each other

130
00:10:28,699 --> 00:10:32,699
And the only way that they're going to be able to get together is by having somebody drive them.

131
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,699
That means that parents and adults are going to get involved with that,

132
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:44,520
And this is going to have a pretty significant influence on when and where patrols can meet and the involvement of adults in those meetings.

133
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:52,280
I think we can also all accept that scout age boys are a lot more closely scheduled with formal activities than they once were.

134
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:56,400
I mean, even in my lifetime that's changed pretty exponentially.

135
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:06,040
Their discretionary time is limited by the demands of homework too, which seems to have grown like crazy since I was in middle school and high school.

136
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,439
I mean, in middle school and high school, homework was fairly unknown,

137
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:12,660
To me at least.

138
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:17,760
Maybe that's why I didn't get very good grades.

139
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:18,260
Who knows,

140
00:11:18,260 --> 00:11:31,120
These challenges of place and time in our era have caused patrol meetings and outings independent from the troop and from adult oversight to be really very rare,

141
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,140
And this is being borne out.

142
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:38,700
I mentioned this survey that I'm working with this online survey that I'm doing about the patrol system,

143
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:45,500
And one of the questions is: you know how often do your patrols meet independent of the troop?

144
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:51,820
By a good margin, the most common answer is they never operate independently from the troop.

145
00:11:52,800 --> 00:12:00,560
We know that scouts are going to benefit from operating independently from the troop, just with their patrol.

146
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:06,060
I think we believe in that sort of thing, but we're stymied by the logistics of carrying it out

147
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:16,800
Now. one promising strategy in addressing these logistical problems is replacing existing troop activities with those that are based on patrols functioning independently,

148
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:19,739
And there's two different ways that this can happen.

149
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:29,520
One is to take a couple of the troop meetings you already have scheduled every month and to change them around and make them exclusively patrol meeting nights:

150
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:35,000
No troop opening, no senior patrol leader running things or anything like that.

151
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:37,400
The scouts show up, they work with their patrol.

152
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:42,439
It's up to their patrol leader to go ahead and plan activities for them,

153
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:47,780
And it doesn't necessarily have to be meeting right there in the same place that every other patrol is meeting.

154
00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:56,420
Maybe there's a bowling alley, maybe there's a park, a pizza parlor, something like that nearby where they can go ahead and get together as a patrol.

155
00:12:56,420 --> 00:13:04,020
The other way of dealing with this is to take a couple of troop campouts every year and turn them into patrol campouts,

156
00:13:04,579 --> 00:13:09,819
where patrols are going to be doing totally different activities from one another,

157
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:18,740
where they'll probably be sited in the same camping space or park or maybe at the scout camp or something like that,

158
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:20,560
But they'll have their own campsite,

159
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:22,380
They'll be off on their own,

160
00:13:22,699 --> 00:13:25,560
They'll be doing their own activities that their patrol has planned for them.

161
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:30,339
The other piece is making sure that you know our standard camping weekend,

162
00:13:30,939 --> 00:13:37,900
that there's a good big chunk of time dedicated for patrols to do things that they're interested in doing,

163
00:13:38,219 --> 00:13:44,819
that their patrol leader is prepared for them and that they can go ahead and pursue without the rest of the troop looking on.

164
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:50,000
In thinking about this, I went back to some of Baden-Powell's writings

165
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:54,380
and I wanted to find out what his thinking was along these lines.

166
00:13:54,380 --> 00:13:56,040
And here's what I found

167
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:57,720
He wrote,

169
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:48,280
And that's what Baden-Powell wrote in the year of 1910..

170
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:53,140
He saw that patrols were essential to scouting-

171
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:55,400
You can't have scouting without them-

172
00:14:56,199 --> 00:15:00,560
And he also said that patrols should be kept intact under all circumstances.

173
00:15:01,199 --> 00:15:07,060
Well, there's still a lot of things to figure out in how to take those broad underlying concepts

174
00:15:07,060 --> 00:15:09,680
and apply them to the conditions that we have today.

175
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:11,219
But I think we're equal to the task

176
00:15:11,959 --> 00:15:16,020
We started a year or so ago, and I know that I've mentioned this before,

177
00:15:16,119 --> 00:15:22,339
but we started a year or so ago replacing one or two of our troop meetings every month with patrol meetings,

178
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:29,060
And the patrol leaders have the entire responsibility for planning those meetings and making them happen.

179
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:36,160
The other thing we've done is we've built into our normal camping schedule very independent patrol-type activities.

180
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:42,119
One that I thought was particularly successful is we got to our scout camp on a Friday night

181
00:15:42,579 --> 00:15:45,420
and we all camped together as a troop on that Friday night.

182
00:15:45,780 --> 00:15:50,199
Saturday morning the scouts all packed their stuff up, put it in their backpacks

183
00:15:50,439 --> 00:15:54,800
and they each headed to a different area of camp to set up their patrol site.

184
00:15:54,800 --> 00:16:00,599
Now we went and looked in on them once or twice during the day on Saturday

185
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:07,959
and they stayed in their own patrol campsite, at a good distance from all the other patrols, overnight on Saturday night

186
00:16:08,040 --> 00:16:11,699
and then they hiked back to a pickup point on Sunday morning and it went beautifully

187
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:16,560
And the scouts really, really enjoyed it and there were absolutely no problem.

188
00:16:16,839 --> 00:16:21,400
That's just some ideas and some underlying concepts for you to put into action.

189
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:23,579
I'd love to hear how you're doing that.

190
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:28,060
I know that, at least according to some of the survey answers that I've seen,

191
00:16:28,480 --> 00:16:34,420
that people are actively supporting independent patrol activities and outings and things,

192
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:36,700
and I'd love to hear how you're making that happen.

